title:   Before the first program
level:   first steps
learn:   what has to be installed, where it comes from, and why PiST changes none of it
source:  template "GEMDOS hello" as hello.s
needs:   assembler

step  The three pieces
  teach  PiST is the editor, the debugger and the glue; it is not the assembler
         and not the emulator. Three things have to be on the machine: vasmm68k_mot
         assembles your source, Hatari runs the resulting ST program, and an EmuTOS
         image gives Hatari a TOS to boot. Two of them are not free software in the
         usual sense — vasm permits redistribution unmodified and for non-commercial
         use — which is why PiST drives them as separate programs through their own
         command lines and never patches or relinks them.
  nominal nothing in the machine changes here; this step is orientation

step  Putting them in place
  teach  View ▸ Tool setup shows what is found and offers to fetch what is not: the
         assembler as the author's source tarball, built here, and EmuTOS as a
         download. The URL and its checksum are shown before anything is fetched, so
         you are never asked to trust a silent download. The welcome view says the
         same thing in one line when something is missing.
  nominal the dialog reports readiness; there is no machine state to check yet

step  Prove the assembler works
  teach  This step builds the file the lesson created for you — the same GEMDOS
         hello program, assembled from disk with nothing else in the way. If the
         Problems pane stays empty, the toolchain is real and working, which is a
         better answer than a green tick in a settings dialog.
  do     build
  expect problems == none
